      "id": 250545087,
      "body": "To be honest Tim, Hormuz's oil crisis doesnt guarantee 200 dollars a barrel instantly, the argument is more: if the Hormuz is closed as it is right now, the issue becomes physical barrels, not the rig count or trend of the chart.\n\nMy cousin who is a Petroleum Engineer brought up rig counts, and in a normal market that would be true but its not a best indicator when a chokepoint is being squeezed out. Rig counts only tell you about the activity of future production, mostly in the US, it does not resolve 20% of the global flow of oil being a risk through a singular chokepoint. The key point Im trying to argue is that its not just news, 20 million barrels per day move through it and each day 13 million barrels are missing. Even with the reroute Saudia Arabia is pulling off + UAE's current situation, there is a huge amount of oil barrels that cant be replaced. If the 13 million barrels remain effectively shut, that means everyday the closure lasts, the market is accumulating a deficit thats physical. Inventories and SPR can help make a small cushion but it wont do much and prices will not instantly explode initially since oil takes time to move. Inventories are not infinite and reserves need political decisions to release, you can try and soften the shock but you cant pretend theres no barrels missing. Saying the \"trend is down\" misses how oil works as a commodity. Oil never prices in the full risk until the physical tightness of oil begins to show itself inventory wise, a market like what we saw in most of april can look calm until many commercials stocks start to draw hard which can lead to massive spikes. I agree 200 isnt guaranteed, but 130-150 is definitely on the Horizon, there is no ceiling to how high oil can go since demand destruction begins to take place as this shortage continues. A prolonged hormuz disruption is exactly the type of event that can break a trend because its not a sentiment issue at all like the markets, its purely a physical supply gutting. I think the best way to phrase this is: the longer the Hormuz is closed, the higher the probability of us seeing a major oil spike, if it reopens then the spike can didsapear, if it stays closed for the remainder of May, 130+ becomes extremely realistic.\n\nI always personally like hearing both sides, and yes for many people who cant handle volatility. This field is where people can get shaken up and will probably get kicked out before they see a profit. Nice post overall, and I appreciate your perspective.",
